The music player (../music): one fix to Chocolate Doom's OPL library, which
is otherwise compiled as it is. It is not part of the engine.

opl_sdl.c keeps its callback queue in "music time", the time less the time
spent paused (SetCallback pushes current_time - pause_offset + us), but
AdjustCallbacks rescaled the queue around current_time. After any pause,
the first tempo change then moved every pending event by about the pause
times (1 - old tempo / new tempo): a song started after a pause, which
sets its tempo at once, stayed silent for most of the pause's length or
longer. Rescaling around music time keeps the queue in its own frame.

diff --git a/opl/opl_sdl.c b/opl/opl_sdl.c
--- a/opl/opl_sdl.c
+++ b/opl/opl_sdl.c
@@ -498,6 +498,6 @@
 static void OPL_SDL_AdjustCallbacks(float factor)
 {
     SDL_LockMutex(callback_queue_mutex);
-    OPL_Queue_AdjustCallbacks(callback_queue, current_time, factor);
+    OPL_Queue_AdjustCallbacks(callback_queue, current_time - pause_offset, factor);
     SDL_UnlockMutex(callback_queue_mutex);
 }
